import Mathlib

namespace Erdos696

/-
# Problem Description

Erdős Problem 696. For almost all `n`, the maximal prime-chain length `h(n)` is
asymptotic to `log* n / 2`, the maximal divisor-chain length `H(n)` is asymptotic to
`log* n`, and their ratio tends to two. `erdos_696` proves all three.

`erdos_696` is proved unconditionally here: the Siegel--Walfisz theorem, previously assumed
in this repository as `siegel_walfisz`, is discharged by `siegelWalfisz_unconditional`,
derived from the Bombieri--Vinogradov development in the vendored `BoundedGaps` library.
Mertens and Brun--Titchmarsh, the other two analytic inputs, are likewise proved
(`AnalyticInputs`). The conditional argument itself is preserved unchanged in
`Conditional`.

The formalisation is by plby (github.com/plby/lean-proofs),
`src/latest/ErdosProblems/Erdos696.lean`, following the conditional argument recorded at
erdosproblems.com/696 (post 6848). It rests on the `BoundedGaps` library of
frenzymath/FormalPantheon and on three `UnitFractions/ForMathlib` helper modules from the
same repository.

Flattened single-file vendoring of the 247-module import closure (227 of them from
`BoundedGaps`), in dependency order, with project-internal imports removed so that
`Mathlib` is the only import, each module wrapped in its own `section` with any
end-of-file scopes closed explicitly. Declarations keep their upstream names. The
`UnitFractions/ForMathlib` modules declare helper lemmas into Mathlib's own `Nat`, `Int`,
`Finset` and `Real` namespaces, so those are emitted with explicit `_root_.` prefixes
rather than nested. The outer `Erdos696` namespace is stripped from the modules that carry
it, since the whole file is wrapped in it once. Names declared in more than one upstream
module — legal there because they are `private` and so module-scoped — are suffixed
`_v1`/`_v2`/... here. No mathematical content is changed.
-/

